\section{The interactive console}{

\code{peter()} without a prompt opens a console when someone can answer (interactive R or
IRkernel); \code{peter(.stdin = TRUE)} reads its input from standard input, and
\code{session |> peter()} opens it on \code{session}. With no explicit or configured model, the
interactive console first offers Codex CLI, Claude Code CLI or API configuration
instructions. Choosing an available CLI saves its default model at user scope; cancelling
returns to R. Piped input skips this setup.
Each input is one of:
\itemize{
\item a prompt, sent as \code{session |> peter("...")} with \code{{name}} interpolation; \verb{@file} adds
the head of a file (at most 40 lines) and \verb{@object} attaches an object by name;
\item \code{!code}: R code run in the session's environment; the code and its output go with the next
prompt (at most 300 estimated tokens). \code{!!code} omits that note. Both execute your own R
immediately, without the model's permission gate or a model request. Workspace changes
remain visible to later calls, and enabled transcripts can record the code; \verb{!!} is not a
privacy boundary. A fenced block marked \code{r}, \code{R}, \code{{r}} or unmarked works like \code{!code}
(other fences are prompt text), a \verb{"""} block is a multi-line prompt, and a trailing
backslash continues a line;
\item a slash command such as \verb{/model}, \verb{/mode}, \verb{/cost} or \verb{/exit}; \verb{/help} lists them all.
}

In CRAN's R for macOS in a terminal a pasted block is one input: its lines join the first one
and \code{... } waits for Enter (text typed there is added); a pasted fence or \verb{"""} block is read
up to its closer at once, and a block pasted at an approval or a choice is dropped and the
question asked again. A last pasted line without a newline waits for Enter at the next prompt,
so a two-line paste without a final newline answers with its first line. Elsewhere (Homebrew
or conda R, Linux terminals, RStudio, R.app, Positron, Windows) pasted lines arrive one at a
time; wrap a multi-line paste in \verb{"""}. Lines typed, pasted or run from a script before a
question (an approval, a choice, the pause menu) appeared are discarded and never answer it:
answer after the prompt.

In a terminal (terminal R or an IDE's Terminal tab; not Windows) a line typed while an answer
runs steers it: the agent reads it at its next step, after the current tool results, and
lines typed or pasted together are one message. The terminal does not echo while an answer
runs: a line shows once you press Enter, in its notice, so it never mixes into the answer.
\code{type_ahead = "follow_up"} (\code{\link[=gptr_config]{gptr_config()}})
queues them for when the agent would stop instead. \verb{/command} and \code{!code} lines typed then
run at the next prompt; an interrupt drops them. On the CLI routes a steer reaches the model
after the CLI's current response. The RStudio, Positron and R.app consoles, and type-ahead at
R's own prompt during \code{peter("...")}, keep typed lines for the next input.

Tab completes R names and file paths after \code{!} in terminal R, R in an IDE's Terminal tab
included; RStudio's console completes R code at the prompt too; radian and Positron's console
offer no completion there.

Ctrl-C while an answer runs opens a pause menu in supported terminal front ends:
\verb{[s]teer} (delivered after the current tool results), \verb{[f]ollow-up}, \verb{[c]ontinue}, \verb{[a]bort}
and, for a foreground call (including a chat turn) when available, \verb{[b]ackground};
a second Ctrl-C aborts. Backgrounding returns to the chat prompt; use
\code{!!gptr::gptr_wait(gptr::gptr_last())} there to wait for completion. Returning to the prompt
does not guarantee progress while the chat waits for input. Under Rscript, background runs
advance only inside blocking gptr calls.
Front ends that cannot resume use an abort fallback. \verb{/exit}, two consecutive interrupts at
the prompt or the end of piped input return the session invisibly, so \code{session = peter()}
keeps the conversation. Use Ctrl-C in a terminal, an IDE's Terminal tab included; the RStudio,
R.app, RGui and Positron consoles use Esc.

Options: \code{gptr.verbose} (0 silent, 1 progress, 2 streamed console, 3 debug; default 0 in
knitr and testthat, 1 under Rscript, 2 at the console), \code{gptr.max_turns_console}
(turns per console prompt, default 200) and \code{gptr.history} (console inputs to R's history,
default \code{TRUE}). Ordinary progress uses normal output; actual errors use stderr.
\code{vignette("interactive-console", package = "gptr")} gives input examples and the complete
built-in command table.
}

\section{Sessions and results}{

A pipe such as \code{session |> peter("Explain the result.")} adds work to the same session
and kept workspace. Use \code{\link[=gptr_fork]{gptr_fork()}} to branch explicitly. The final answer is
\code{session$text}; \code{session$value} is the R value selected by \code{\link[=gptr_return]{gptr_return()}} during a tool
call. Agent-created objects remain in \code{envir}.
}

